import{_ as t,o as a,c as s,ag as o}from"./chunks/framework.D1z9wg1y.js";const p=JSON.parse('{"title":"Leak checks and gitleaks","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"safeguards/leaks.md","filePath":"safeguards/leaks.md"}'),n={name:"safeguards/leaks.md"};function h(i,e,r,l,d,c){return a(),s("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[o('<h1 id="leak-checks-and-gitleaks" tabindex="-1">Leak checks and gitleaks <a class="header-anchor" href="#leak-checks-and-gitleaks" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Leak checks and gitleaks&quot;">​</a></h1><p>A public repository keeps every string that a push sends to it, also after a later commit removes the string. The harness is such a repository, and it is written in a private research tree whose names, paths and secrets must stay out of it. So every push of the harness passes two checks: gitleaks, which finds secrets and home paths, and <code>harness leaks</code>, which finds the private strings of your profile. This page describes both checks, where they run, and what they do not find.</p><h2 id="two-kinds-of-leak" tabindex="-1">Two kinds of leak <a class="header-anchor" href="#two-kinds-of-leak" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Two kinds of leak&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left;">kind</th><th style="text-align:left;">an example</th><th style="text-align:left;">found by</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><strong>a secret</strong></td><td style="text-align:left;">a token, a key, a password in a configuration file</td><td style="text-align:left;">gitleaks, with its default rules</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><strong>a home path</strong></td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>/Users/&lt;name&gt;/…</code> in a log that went into a test fixture</td><td style="text-align:left;">gitleaks, with the rule <code>home-path</code> of <code>.gitleaks.toml</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><strong>a private string</strong></td><td style="text-align:left;">the name of a private project, a host, an institution</td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>harness leaks</code>, with the profile&#39;s <code>leak</code> list</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><strong>a name of the research tree</strong></td><td style="text-align:left;">the name of a repository of your tree, or of an organisation</td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>harness leaks</code>, from the profile&#39;s <code>repository_roots</code> and <code>org</code></td></tr></tbody></table><p>gitleaks knows the shapes of secrets, but not your private names. <code>harness leaks</code> knows your names, but not the shapes of secrets. Each check covers what the other cannot.</p><h2 id="gitleaks" tabindex="-1">gitleaks <a class="header-anchor" href="#gitleaks" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;gitleaks&quot;">​</a></h2><p><a href="./../components/other#gitleaks-toml"><code>.gitleaks.toml</code></a> keeps the default rules of gitleaks and adds the rule <code>home-path</code>, a path below <code>/Users/</code> or <code>/home/</code> that names a user. Only the two example homes of the repository, <code>/Users/me</code> and <code>/home/example</code>, pass. Every call passes <code>--redact</code>, so a finding does not print the secret, and <code>--ignore-gitleaks-allow</code>, so a <code>gitleaks:allow</code> comment in a file has no effect. gitleaks 8.21 or later is needed; CI pins 8.30.1.</p><p>Remove a false positive by a change of the text, not of the configuration. A new allowlist entry needs your approval and its own line in the changelog.</p><h2 id="harness-leaks" tabindex="-1"><code>harness leaks</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#harness-leaks" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;`harness leaks`&quot;">​</a></h2><p><a href="./../harness-command#leaks"><code>harness leaks</code></a> searches the tracked files of the harness and their paths for two sets of words:</p><ul><li>the strings of the profile&#39;s <code>leak</code> list, anywhere in a line;</li><li>the names of the research tree, each as a whole word: each directory below each of the profile&#39;s <code>repository_roots</code>, and each organisation of its <code>org</code> list.</li></ul><p>It also renders the templates with the example profile <code>examples/profile.toml</code> and searches the result for the <code>leak</code> strings, so a template that would write a private string into an installed file is found too. With <code>--commits FILE</code>, it also searches the paths and the lines that each commit of the file adds, and each commit&#39;s message.</p><div class="language-bash vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">bash</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">harness</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> leaks</span></span></code></pre></div><p>It exits 0 with no hit, 1 on a hit, and 2 when the profile does not load. Run it before a commit of the harness; <a href="./../daily-use#the-leak-check-before-a-push">Typical use</a> shows the steps.</p><h2 id="where-the-checks-run" tabindex="-1">Where the checks run <a class="header-anchor" href="#where-the-checks-run" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Where the checks run&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left;">where</th><th style="text-align:left;">on</th><th style="text-align:left;">runs</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:left;">the pre-push hook of the harness</td><td style="text-align:left;">a push to <code>main</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">gitleaks over the commits of the push, gitleaks over their messages on standard input, gitleaks over the tree at the pushed commit, then <code>harness leaks --commits</code> over the tree and the commits</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;">the pre-push hook of the harness</td><td style="text-align:left;">a push of another branch</td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>harness leaks --commits</code> only</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;">CI, the job <code>leaks</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">every push and every pull request</td><td style="text-align:left;">a canary first, a synthetic token and home path that gitleaks must find and redact; then gitleaks over the pushed range and its messages, over the checkout, and over the templates rendered with the example profile</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;">you</td><td style="text-align:left;">before a commit</td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>harness leaks</code></td></tr></tbody></table><p><strong>The hook fails closed.</strong> No gitleaks of 8.21 or later, no profile, an empty <code>leak</code> list, or a check that fails, each refuses the push with one line. The hook runs on a <code>git clone --shared</code> of the pushed commit, so it checks what it pushes, not the working tree. Then it runs the suite. <a href="./../development#the-pre-push-hook">Development</a> describes the hook in full.</p><p><strong>CI has no profile.</strong> It runs gitleaks, but it cannot search for your private strings, because it does not know them. The hook on your machine is the one check of the private strings.</p><h2 id="the-token-check-of-the-guard" tabindex="-1">The token check of the guard <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-token-check-of-the-guard" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The token check of the guard&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The guard package has a check of its own, <a href="./guard-hooks#the-token-check"><code>token_forms</code></a>, which is independent of the two checks above. It reads each outgoing shell command, web call and Kaimon call before it runs, and refuses one that holds a token of a known form of GitHub, GitLab or an <code>sk-</code> provider, as it is or percent-decoded. It prevents an accident, such as a token pasted into a command. It is no boundary: a token that is encoded in another way, split, or read from a file by a script passes it.</p><h2 id="the-limits" tabindex="-1">The limits <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-limits" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The limits&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>A push with the hook off is not checked on your machine.</strong> In a clone where <code>core.hooksPath</code> is not set, the hook does not run; CI still runs gitleaks.</li><li><strong>A private string that is not in the <code>leak</code> list is not found.</strong> Add each new private name to the list.</li><li><strong>Some parts of a commit are not searched:</strong> its author and committer, a tag message, and <code>git notes</code>.</li><li><strong>Known issues:</strong> a whole-history gitleaks scan is red until the history is rewritten (K36); <code>useDefault = true</code> brings the default global path allowlists of gitleaks into the configuration (K37); <code>gitleaks git</code> exits 0 on a range that it cannot read (K38); the rule <code>home-path</code> finds a macOS home path but no Linux home path (K81); six branches of the pre-push hook have no case that fails when they change (K112).</li></ul><p><strong>In another repository.</strong> The two checks fit any public repository that is written in a private tree. gitleaks needs only its configuration. <code>harness leaks</code> needs a profile with a <code>leak</code> list.</p>',23)])])}const k=t(n,[["render",h]]);export{p as __pageData,k as default};
